<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Tusculum</h1><p class="st-meta">999 CE – 1179 CE (approx.) · capital Tusculum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Lordship of the Counts of Tusculum, a Roman noble family who controlled the papacy in the early 11th century and fought the commune of Rome.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tusculum">Tusculum</a> descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophylacti">Theophylacti</a>, the family that dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 10th century. Around 999 Gregory I of Tusculum held the fortified hill town of Tusculum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a>, and from there his family built a lordship over the countryside south-east of Rome.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Tusculani controlled papal elections for decades: Gregory's sons became Popes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-benedict-viii">Benedict VIII</a> (1012–1024) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xix">John XIX</a> (1024–1032), and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-benedict-ix">Benedict IX</a> was pope three times before being removed. Their power declined after the reform <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the Roman commune emerged, but in 1167 Tusculan troops and imperial forces routed a Roman army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-monte-porzio">Monte Porzio</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Squeezed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, the counts placed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tusculum">Tusculum</a> under papal and imperial protection in the 12th century. In 1191 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Henry VI</a>'s consent, destroyed the town. Survivors are said to have founded nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frascati">Frascati</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-tusculum.jpg" alt="County of Tusculum" width="320" height="372"><figcaption>Image: Ddurbmonnejg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_Counts_of_Tusculum.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>999 CE – 1179 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tusculum</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gregory I of Tusculum, Alberic III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Counts of Tusculum, Tusculani</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1089&amp;focus=county-of-tusculum" aria-label="County of Tusculum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4989 689 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Tusculum lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5309 945L5304 942M5309 945L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5309 945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5309 945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tusculum. <a href="/atlas?year=1089&amp;focus=county-of-tusculum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

